    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm specifically trying to model the failure points when the heat soak becomes excessive. In some of our test cycles, the braking performance seems to become unpredictable or erratic shortly after the thermal limits are reached, almost as if some external factor is causing the friction coefficient to fluctuate wildly. What are the most common mechanical causes for that kind of erratic behavior during a fade event?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The erratic behavior you are observing during those thermal limit breaches is likely the result of the brake rotor warping due to uneven heat soak, which creates a \"guitar neck\" effect where the rotor bows out of true. When the rotor warps, the pad-to-rotor contact becomes intermittent, causing the friction coefficient to fluctuate wildly as the pad slaps against the metal surface rather than gliding smoothly. This mechanical instability is often exacerbated by the thermal expansion of the semi-metallic friction material itself, which can cause the pad to expand unevenly and lose its structural integrity under extreme heat.\n\nIn the context of your modeling, you should also account for the \"glazing\" phenomenon, where the friction surface becomes overly smooth and shiny, effectively turning the brake pad into a slippery ice rink. This is particularly common with semi-metallic compounds that have higher thermal conductivity but lower heat capacity compared to carbon-ceramic alternatives. When the heat soak becomes excessive, the glazing effect can cause the friction coefficient to drop precipitously, leading to the unpredictable deceleration you are seeing.\n\nTo accurately model these failure points, consider incorporating the thermal lag between the pad and the rotor, as well as the potential for gas entrapment",
      "source": "target",
